# Local credentials — never commit
.bench.env
*.env
!.env.example

# Vendored tool binaries and language runtimes for the benchmark arms. Large,
# platform-specific and rebuildable — each arm's REPRODUCE.md says how to fetch
# them, and benchmarks/agent-env/ now provisions them into the agent container.
benchmarks/arms/*/vendor/
benchmarks/arms/*/vendor-local/
benchmarks/arms/*/.runtime/
benchmarks/arms/terraform-ec2-multiregion/terraform
benchmarks/arms/*/.terraform/
benchmarks/arms/*/cdk.out/

# Emulator runtime state — recreated by benchmarks/floci/reset.sh.
benchmarks/floci/data/

# prepare.py --export writes to ~/.aws-bench/agent-env. Guarded here too, since
# an in-repo export is ~3.5GB and ruff walks anything inside the tree, including
# the Python that ships inside Node's own share/doc.
benchmarks/agent-env/toolchain/
benchmarks/agent-env/workspaces/

# Deployed estate state. Written by a run, rewritten by the next one, and
# meaningless outside the emulator that produced it — every id in here is
# floci's, against account 000000000000. It churned on every commit all through
# the benchmark work and belongs to a run, not to the repo.
#
# `proof/` is excluded from the exclusion: it is a fixture, not a run artifact.
benchmarks/arms/*/.alchemy/
benchmarks/arms/*/.pulumi/
benchmarks/arms/*/terraform.tfstate
benchmarks/arms/*/terraform.tfstate.backup
benchmarks/arms/*/cdk.out/
!benchmarks/arms/*/proof/**

# Result sets. Emitted per run, and published from chant-bench, which is where
# the contract and the validation live. The copies that were tracked here went
# stale the moment the emitter grew a field: they carried an older harness
# commit, older briefing SHAs, and no cost or tooling blocks at all, while the
# published ones for the same run ids carried all three. Two sources of truth
# for one number, and the wrong one was the one in the repo.
benchmarks/results/
